Special Containment Procedures:  Containment of SCP-5176 instances is currently impossible. A disinformation campaign has been established globally in order to obfuscate SCP-5176's existence. MTF Gamma-5 () are to disseminate and maintain global academic, scientific, and public cover stories describing SCP-5176 as a naturally-occurring variety of octopus, genus Hapalochlaena1. All fabricated records of SCP-5176 are to acknowledge its existence and advise against interaction with the creature. Propaganda advertising a highly toxic bite is to be distributed and maintained by MTF Gamma-5 in order to discourage contact. Periodically, MTF Gamma-5 is to disseminate anecdotes and publicity regarding deaths and near-deaths as a result of being bitten by SCP-5176.

In the event that captive instances of SCP-5176 are reported missing, MTF Gamma-5 are to intervene by substituting these missing instances with genetically-modified Abdopus aculeatens2and administer Class-B amnestics to all civilians reporting similar phenomena.

Individuals reporting sightings of, or communication with SCP-5176-1 instances are to be intercepted by MTF Gamma-5 and administered with class-B amnestics. In the event that SCP-5176-1 manifests in a public aquarium, MTF Gamma-5 is to covertly furnish replacements for all consumed organisms in addition to amnestic application.

SCP-5176-3 instances are inert objects preserved in Site-██ via isopropyl.

Description:  SCP-5176 are entities resembling small aquatic organisms of the order Octopoda. While displaying anatomical and behavioral attributes consistent with members of this order, SCP-5176 is entirely distinct genetically.

While SCP-5176 possess the natural ability to camouflage within its surroundings, they also display distinctive blue-ringed patterns when approached by human subjects. In isolation, these patterns have a minor memetic calming affect on human subjects.

SCP-5176 are capable of instantaneous teleportation between bodies of water over theoretically infinite distances. Upon relocation, the Dyson-Powell readings in the water surrounding the previous location and new location both rise by approximately .334 DPK.3 Further research concludes that SCP-5176 are traveling trans-dimensionally during relocation. The reason for the apparent restriction of SCP-5176's teleportation to bodies of water is currently unknown.

SCP-5176 do not appear to require sustenance, however, they have shown a strong attraction to many forms of aquatic wildlife, including crabs, other octopods, and all observed species of fish. Individual instances rarely possess the ability to subdue fish species larger than six centimeters, however instances of SCP-5176 have been observed unsuccessfully attempting to pursue and feed on fish as large as Carcharodon carcharias4.

On occasion, multiple instances may relocate to one location and conglomerate into a single amorphous mass. This entity, hereby referred to as SCP-5176-1, may contain up to approximately two thousand instances bound together by loose mucous membranes. Research indicates that SCP-5176-1 is more adept at swimming and predation than isolated SCP-5176 instances. SCP-5176-1 has been recorded to successfully attack and completely consume entire shoals of Scombridae6.

This predation is aided by the combined pulsation of the blue-ring patterns of all composite instances. This combined effect has been shown to be a memetic reassurance agent, and is especially potent on aquatic species. The effect has been observed to be less efficient on humans, however subjects still reported feelings of calm, relaxation, and dulled thinking when in SCP-5176-1's presence.

SCP-5176-1 displays higher levels of sapience than isolated SCP-5176. When addressed verbally, SCP-5176-1 may respond by writing messages with a pseudopod in ink emanating from individual SCP-5176 instances. Communication is thus possible by furnishing an aquarium with large amounts of fish to entice SCP-5176-1, and then projecting researcher questions into containment via loudspeakers. SCP-5176-1 has never remained in containment for more than an hour.

Historical evidence dates SCP-5176 activity to at least the early 18th century. Documents from this period that describe SCP-5176 have been lifted by the Foundation from a historic library in Carcosa, Australia.

SCP-5176-1 speaks Portuguese, and will only respond legibly to questions phrased in this language.

